/* book exam traps: EMC is two-sided: low emission and enough immunity. Answers that only describe suppression of the ignition miss half the topic. | A freewheeling diode must be fitted with its cathode to the positive end of the coil. Fitted the other way, it shorts the supply when the relay is switched on. | A freewheeling diode slows the release of a relay. Where fast release matters, a Zener or resistor is added in series with the diode. | Capacitive coupling grows with dv/dt and inductive coupling with di/dt; mixing them up gives the wrong remedy (screening against electric fields, twisting and small loops against magnetic fields). | Resistor spark plugs and suppression leads reduce radio interference, not spark energy needed for ignition; replacing them with plain plugs is a common cause of radio noise. */
