/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A cell is one complete chemical unit: two plates with the electrolyte between them.
   - In series the same current flows through every cell and the cell voltages add.
   - n is the number of cells in series (6 in an automotive battery); V_cell is the open-circuit voltage of one fully charged cell, about 2.1 V; V_battery is the battery terminal voltage at rest, in volts.
   - The series connection gives the 12.6 V the starter needs (see Chapter 1.9); the large plate area and the few milliohms of internal resistance let the pack push out hundreds of amperes, with the terminal voltage sagging by a volt or two.
   - A cell is a stack of alternating plates.
   - Plates of one polarity are welded to a strap to form a plate group; a positive and a negative group interleave with separators to form the plate block of one cell, and cell connectors pass through the partitions to join the cells in series.
   - Two plate families exist.
   - A porous separator lies between each pair of opposite plates.
   - The electrolyte is dilute sulfuric acid (H2SO4 in water).
   - Everything sits in one compartment of a hard rubber or polypropylene case.
*/
