/* book Worked example 2: Q: A 12 V, 60 Ah battery has an electrolyte specific gravity of 1.28 when fully charged. Use the rule cell voltage ≈ SG + 0.84. (a) Find the rested battery voltage when full and when the SG has fallen to 1.20. (b) The battery has delivered 30 Ah. Each ampere-hour that passes through a cell uses 3.66 g of sulfuric acid. Find the acid used in one cell and in the whole battery.
   1. Full: cell voltage = 1.28 + 0.84 = 2.12 V; battery = 6 × 2.12 V = 12.72 V.
   2. Part-discharged: cell voltage = 1.20 + 0.84 = 2.04 V; battery = 6 × 2.04 V = 12.24 V, close to the 12.2 V half-charge reading used in battery testing.
   3. Where 3.66 g/Ah comes from: 1 Ah = 3600 C = 0.0373 mol of electrons (3600 ÷ 96,485), and the reaction uses one H₂SO₄ per electron: 0.0373 × 98.1 g = 3.66 g.
   4. One cell: the same 30 Ah flows through every cell in series, so acid used = 30 Ah × 3.66 g/Ah = 110 g.
   5. Whole battery: 6 cells × 110 g = 659 g of acid turned into lead sulfate.
   Answer: The battery rests at about 12.72 V when full and 12.24 V at SG 1.20; delivering 30 Ah uses about 110 g of acid in each cell, about 659 g in the whole battery. */
