/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Ampere-hour (Ah) is stored charge.
   - I is constant current in A; t is discharge time in h; Ah is ampere-hour capacity.
   - At the 20 h rate, I = Ah ÷ 20 h.
   - Reserve capacity (RC) is the time in minutes a fully charged battery at about 25 °C can supply 25 A before terminal voltage falls to 10.5 V.
   - Cold cranking amps (CCA) is the current a battery can deliver at −18 °C for 30 s while keeping at least 7.2 V across its terminals (the SAE definition).
   - Ah is not starting current.
   - Open-circuit voltage (OCV) is measured after the battery rests, with no load.
   - A hydrometer measures specific gravity (SG) of the electrolyte.
   - Safety: a charging battery makes hydrogen gas, which explodes with a spark.
   - A load test heats the battery; let it rest before repeating.
*/
