/* book Worked example 2: Q: Two 12 V batteries are rated CCA 450 A and reserve capacity 100 min. (a) In a load test at half the CCA for 15 s, battery A falls from 12.6 V to 10.1 V and battery B from 12.6 V to 9.2 V. Find the load current and the internal resistance of each, and say which passes (limit 9.6 V). (b) In a reserve-capacity test at 25 A, battery B reaches 10.5 V after 72 min. Find the charge it delivered and its reserve capacity as a percentage of the rating.
   1. Load test current: I = CCA ÷ 2 = 450 A ÷ 2 = 225 A.
   2. Battery A: drop = 12.6 V − 10.1 V = 2.5 V, so R = 2.5 V ÷ 225 A = 0.0111 Ω = 11.1 mΩ. 10.1 V is above 9.6 V: it passes.
   3. Battery B: drop = 12.6 V − 9.2 V = 3.4 V, so R = 3.4 V ÷ 225 A = 0.0151 Ω = 15.1 mΩ. 9.2 V is below 9.6 V: it fails.
   4. Reserve test of B: charge delivered Q = I × t = 25 A × (72 ÷ 60) h = 30 Ah.
   5. A battery that meets its rating delivers 25 A × (100 ÷ 60) h = 41.7 Ah in the same test.
   6. Percentage of rated reserve capacity = 72 min ÷ 100 min × 100 = 72 %.
   Answer: The test current is 225 A; battery A (11.1 mΩ, 10.1 V) passes and battery B (15.1 mΩ, 9.2 V) fails. B delivered 30 Ah in 72 min, only 72 % of its rated reserve capacity, so it should be replaced. */
